@flexiarg workshop/revise-patterns-from-observed-use
@title Revise Patterns from Observed Use
@keywords maintenance, revision, outcomes, cascade, sanctions, cooling-off
@audience pattern authors, affected participants, reviewers, Layer 4 workshop authority
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade technique
@provenance Joe requested a Layer 4 process for managing pattern-language extensions, 2026-09-12; codex-25 operationalization of the discussion of Corneli (2016), An institutional approach to computational social creativity, https://metameso.org/~joe/papers/corneli2016institutional.pdf. Proposed local procedure, not a quotation from the paper.
@review Joe authorized adding this process layer; Joe alone currently adopts workshop-family institutional rules and sanctions. Affected participants advise and challenge; they do not thereby acquire adoption authority.
@class process
@domain process
@family-note workshop/ Layer 4 extension and maintenance process; see LAYER4-PROCESS.md for the linked sequence.
@status authored 2026-09-12; not assayed; no runtime enforcement claimed.

! conclusion:
  Use observed outcomes to retain, revise, reorganize, or retire patterns;
  make the change in the authoritative artifacts and preserve the evidence
  and decision that explain it.

  + context:
    An append-only library can accumulate conflicting advice while its
    users repeatedly encounter the same difficulty. Reports that a lesson
    was adopted can coexist with unchanged authoritative text.

  + IF:
    A bounded use or review supplies evidence that a pattern was followed,
    not followed, misapplied, ineffective, or in conflict with another pattern.

  + HOWEVER:
    A poor outcome does not establish disobedience, and disobedience does
    not establish that the rule was sound. Outcomes may have other causes.
    Missing application evidence is unknown, not proof of noncompliance.

  + THEN:
    The reviewing agent records the pattern version, situation, evidence
    of following or departing from it, observed outcome, and uncertainty
    about the connection. Classify the finding before proposing a response:
    application error, scope mismatch, ineffective advice, rule conflict,
    apparatus fault, or insufficient evidence. Propose retention, narrowing,
    revision, merge, split, retirement, or changed cascade relationships;
    name an owner and a review event for unresolved findings.
    Route substantive changes through workshop/open-proposals-named-adoption
    and workshop/distinguishing-cases-before-adoption. Apply an adopted
    change to the authoritative text and affected references, preserve the
    prior version, and have a reviewer compare the resulting artifacts with
    the decision. Where a sigil registry entry pins the changed pattern,
    update its pin to the adopted version and verify that it resolves to
    those exact bytes. Preserve historical pins on historical evidence;
    do not silently retarget earlier claims. An unavailable registry update
    remains an open maintenance gap.
    A response paragraph alone does not complete the revision.
    If the finding suggests a sanction or cooling-off period, record it as
    a proposal with its affected privilege, grounds, decision authority,
    duration or release condition, challenge route, and restoration review.
    This pattern itself authorizes no penalty or automatic suspension.

  + BECAUSE:
    The commons improves when experience changes usable guidance and its
    relationships, rather than merely increasing document count. Separating
    diagnosis, correction, and sanctions keeps honest reports useful and
    makes future governance extensions reviewable.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Attach the describe-versus-correct finding to workshop/progress-is-a-witnessed-state-change.]
    next[Review reuse, unresolved conflicts, obsolete references, and review effort as library conditions.]
    next[Bring specific sanction proposals through this process when an observed case warrants them.]
